### What is the Service Provider Interface pattern?

The SPI pattern separates three roles:

- **Service** – an interface or abstract class defining a capability.
- **Provider** – a concrete implementation, usually in its own jar.
- **Consumer** – code that needs the capability but should not depend on any specific provider.

`ServiceLoader` connects consumers to providers without a compile-time dependency. This is how the JDK loads JDBC drivers, charset providers, and logging backends.

```java
package com.bank.spi;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// The service interface
public interface FeeCalculator {
    String name();
    BigDecimal calculate(String accountType, BigDecimal amount);
}
```

### How does classpath discovery work?

A provider jar contains a text file at `META-INF/services/com.bank.spi.FeeCalculator`. Each line is the fully-qualified name of an implementation class.

```text
# META-INF/services/com.bank.spi.FeeCalculator
com.vendor.gst.GstFeeCalculator
com.vendor.flat.FlatFeeCalculator
```

```java
package com.vendor.gst;

import com.bank.spi.FeeCalculator;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class GstFeeCalculator implements FeeCalculator {
    @Override
    public String name() {
        return "GST Fee";
    }

    @Override
    public BigDecimal calculate(String accountType, BigDecimal amount) {
        // 18% GST on service fee
        BigDecimal serviceFee = amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.005"));
        return serviceFee.multiply(new BigDecimal("1.18"));
    }
}
```

```java
package com.vendor.flat;

import com.bank.spi.FeeCalculator;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class FlatFeeCalculator implements FeeCalculator {
    private static final BigDecimal FLAT_FEE = new BigDecimal("50.00");

    @Override
    public String name() {
        return "Flat Fee";
    }

    @Override
    public BigDecimal calculate(String accountType, BigDecimal amount) {
        return FLAT_FEE;
    }
}
```

### How does the consumer load providers?

The consumer calls `ServiceLoader.load(FeeCalculator.class)` and iterates. Providers are instantiated lazily as you iterate.

```java
package com.bank.billing;

import com.bank.spi.FeeCalculator;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ServiceLoader;

public class BillingEngine {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal transferAmount = new BigDecimal("25000.00");
        BigDecimal totalFees = BigDecimal.ZERO;

        // Discover and load all fee calculators
        ServiceLoader<FeeCalculator> loader =
            ServiceLoader.load(FeeCalculator.class);

        for (FeeCalculator calc : loader) {
            BigDecimal fee = calc.calculate("savings", transferAmount);
            System.out.println(calc.name() + ": " + fee);
            totalFees = totalFees.add(fee);
        }

        System.out.println("Total fees: " + totalFees);
    }
}
```

```text
GST Fee: 147.50
Flat Fee: 50.00
Total fees: 197.50
```

### Why is lazy loading important?

The loader does not read all files and instantiate everything up front. As you iterate, it locates the next provider, loads the class, and instantiates it one at a time. This lets you:

- Stop after finding a suitable provider.
- Handle per-provider failures without crashing.

```java
// Using stream() for lazy inspection before instantiation
ServiceLoader.load(FeeCalculator.class).stream()
    .filter(p -> p.type().getSimpleName().startsWith("Gst"))
    .map(ServiceLoader.Provider::get)
    .forEach(calc -> System.out.println(calc.name()));
```

### How does discovery work in the module system?

In modules, the text file is replaced by a declarative `provides ... with` in `module-info.java`.

```java
// Provider module
module com.vendor.gst {
    requires com.bank.spi;
    provides com.bank.spi.FeeCalculator
        with com.vendor.gst.GstFeeCalculator;
}

// Consumer module
module com.bank.billing {
    requires com.bank.spi;
    uses com.bank.spi.FeeCalculator;
}
```

The compiler verifies these declarations, so a misspelled provider is caught at build time, not runtime.

### What role does the context class loader play?

By default, `ServiceLoader.load(Service.class)` uses the thread's context class loader. This is the back-channel frameworks use so that discovery can reach application-level providers that strict parent delegation would miss.

```java
// Default: uses thread context class loader
ServiceLoader.load(FeeCalculator.class);

// Explicit class loader for precise control
ServiceLoader.load(FeeCalculator.class, myClassLoader);
```

### What are the provider instantiation rules?

On the classpath, providers must have a public no-argument constructor. The modern alternative is a static `provider()` method that returns an instance.

```java
public class GstFeeCalculator implements FeeCalculator {
    // Option 1: public no-arg constructor (default)
    public GstFeeCalculator() {}

    // Option 2: static provider method
    public static FeeCalculator provider() {
        return new GstFeeCalculator();
    }

    // ... implementation
}
```

### What are the error handling considerations?

Iterating can throw `ServiceConfigurationError` if a declared provider is missing, is not a subtype, or fails to instantiate. Robust consumers iterate defensively.

```java
ServiceLoader<FeeCalculator> loader =
    ServiceLoader.load(FeeCalculator.class);

for (var provider : loader) {
    try {
        BigDecimal fee = provider.calculate("savings", amount);
        System.out.println(provider.name() + ": " + fee);
    } catch (ServiceConfigurationError e) {
        System.err.println("Skipping bad provider: " + e.getMessage());
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Service loading is unordered. If you need priority, add your own ranking via an annotation or a method on the interface, and sort the discovered providers yourself. The payoff is genuine extensibility: you add a capability by dropping a jar or module on the path, with zero changes to the consumer code. This is why JDBC drivers, logging frameworks, and many JDK subsystems use SPI.
